{"answerArea":{"calculator":false,"chi2Table":false,"periodicTable":false,"tTable":false,"zTable":false},"hints":[{"content":"Notice that we can rewrite the integral as $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{1+(e^x)^2}\\cdot \\,e^x\\, dx\\,$.\n\n ","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"If we let $\\blueD {u=e^x}$, then $\\purpleD{du=e^x \\, dx}$.  \n\nSubstituting gives us:\n\n$\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{1+(\\blueD{e^x})^2}\\,\\cdot \\purpleD{e^x\\, dx}\\,=\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{1+\\blueD u^2}\\, \\purpleD{du}\\,$","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"We recognize this antiderivative.\n\n$\\begin{align}\\phantom{\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{e^x}{1+e^{2x}}dx}\u0026= \\int\\dfrac{1}{1+u^2}\\,du~\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=~ \\tan^{-1}u+C\\end{align}$\n\n","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"We can now substitute back to find the antiderivative in terms of $x$.\n\n$\\begin{align}\\phantom{\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{e^x}{1+e^{2x}}dx~}\u0026=~\\tan^{-1}u+C\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=~\\tan^{-1}(e^x)+C\\end{align}$","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"The answer:\n\n$\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{e^x}{1+e^{2x}}dx\\,=~\\tan^{-1}(e^x)+C$","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}}],"itemDataVersion":{"major":0,"minor":1},"question":{"content":" $\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{e^x}{1+e^{2x}}dx\\,=$\n\n[[☃ radio 1]]","images":{},"widgets":{"radio 1":{"alignment":"default","graded":true,"options":{"choices":[{"content":"$\\displaystyle\\ln{(1+e^{2x})+C}$","correct":false},{"content":"$\\displaystyle\\ln{(1+e^{x})+C}$","correct":false},{"content":"$\\displaystyle\\sin^{-1}(e^x)+C$","correct":false},{"content":"$\\displaystyle\\tan^{-1}(e^x)+C$","correct":true}],"deselectEnabled":false,"displayCount":null,"hasNoneOfTheAbove":false,"multipleSelect":false,"onePerLine":true,"randomize":true},"static":false,"type":"radio","version":{"major":1,"minor":0}}}}}
